{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"id\":\"R-25:29\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"25:29 links Satan with leading someone away from remembrance after it arrived; this makes the focus’s diversion sound like betrayal in the presence of guidance.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"25:29\",\"ar\":\"لَّقَدْ أَضَلَّنِى عَنِ ٱلذِّكْرِ بَعْدَ إِذْ جَآءَنِى ۗ وَكَانَ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ لِلْإِنسَٰنِ خَذُولًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَزَيَّنَ لَهُمُ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ فَصَدَّهُمْ عَنِ ٱلسَّبِيلِ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages name Satan and describe a turn away from guidance.\",\"limits\":\"25:29 is a speaker’s lament about a companion; it does not describe the named peoples or the beautification of deeds.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-25:38\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"Al-Furqan repeats the exact pair Âd and Thamud within a wider catalogue of judged peoples; it situates the focus’s names in that catalogue without explaining their dwellings or diversion.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"25:37\",\"ar\":\"وَقَوْمَ نُوحٍۢ لَّمَّا كَذَّبُوا۟ ٱلرُّسُلَ أَغْرَقْنَٰهُمْ وَجَعَلْنَٰهُمْ لِلنَّاسِ ءَايَةًۭ\"},{\"ref\":\"25:38\",\"ar\":\"وَعَادًۭا وَثَمُودَا۟ وَأَصْحَٰبَ ٱلرَّسِّ وَقُرُونًۢا بَيْنَ ذَٰلِكَ كَثِيرًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَعَادًۭا وَثَمُودَا۟\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus resumes the same paired names after its account of earlier destroyed peoples.\",\"limits\":\"The catalogue supplies no further detail about Âd or Thamud here.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-25:39\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The line after the names says each listed people received examples and was utterly ruined; it places the focus’s testimony from dwellings within a warning-and-destruction sequence.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"25:38\",\"ar\":\"وَعَادًۭا وَثَمُودَا۟\"},{\"ref\":\"25:39\",\"ar\":\"وَكُلًّۭا ضَرَبْنَا لَهُ ٱلْأَمْثَٰلَ ۖ وَكُلًّۭا تَبَّرْنَا تَتْبِيرًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"قَد تَّبَيَّنَ لَكُم مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"The preceding verse names Âd and Thamud, and the focus points to their dwellings as evidence.\",\"limits\":\"25:39 does not specify which examples were given or identify the dwellings mentioned in 29:38.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-25:40\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The passage warns that seeing a ruined town does not ensure its lesson is received: its observers see it, yet do not expect resurrection. This sharpens the focus’s claim that the dwellings are clear evidence while the peoples themselves were diverted.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"25:40\",\"ar\":\"أَفَلَمْ يَكُونُوا۟ يَرَوْنَهَا ۚ بَلْ كَانُوا۟ لَا يَرْجُونَ نُشُورًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَقَد تَّبَيَّنَ لَكُم مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ ۖ وَزَيَّنَ لَهُمُ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages bring visible remains together with a failure of moral or eschatological response.\",\"limits\":\"25:40 does not identify its town or its observers with Âd and Thamud.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:30\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"Moses speaks of bringing Pharaoh a manifest proof, paralleling the focus’s description of evidence as already clear from the dwellings.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"26:30\",\"ar\":\"قَالَ أَوَلَوْ جِئْتُكَ بِشَىْءٍۢ مُّبِينٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَقَد تَّبَيَّنَ لَكُم مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"The shared concern is whether a proof is manifest to its audience.\",\"limits\":\"Moses’s proposed proof and the focus’s physical traces are different evidence in different episodes.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"low\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:95\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The phrase “all the armies of Iblis” places satanic affiliation among those cast into Hell, extending the focus’s picture of Satan’s activity toward an end-state among the condemned.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"26:94\",\"ar\":\"فَكُبْكِبُوا۟ فِيهَا هُمْ وَٱلْغَاوُۥنَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:95\",\"ar\":\"وَجُنُودُ إِبْلِيسَ أَجْمَعُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَزَيَّنَ لَهُمُ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus names Satan as the agent of diversion; the other passage names Iblis’s forces among the condemned.\",\"limits\":\"The passage does not identify Âd or Thamud as Iblis’s soldiers.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:97\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"A possible reversal sets the focus’s description of the peoples as مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ beside condemned speakers who confess they were in manifest error; the pairing could distinguish perceptive capacity from a rightly directed path.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"26:97\",\"ar\":\"تَٱللَّهِ إِن كُنَّا لَفِى ضَلَٰلٍۢ مُّبِينٍ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَكَانُوا۟ مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ\"}],\"missing\":\"A lexical account of مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ and an explicit link between these different condemned groups could decide whether this is a meaningful contrast or just a shared theme of error.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:123\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The separate account of Âd states directly that they denied the messengers, adding prophetic rejection to the focus’s description of their diversion from the way.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"26:123\",\"ar\":\"كَذَّبَتْ عَادٌ ٱلْمُرْسَلِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَعَادًۭا وَثَمُودَا۟\"}],\"activation\":\"The same named people appear in both passages, while the focus leaves their specific response unstated.\",\"limits\":\"26:123 names Âd but supplies neither the focus’s Satan language nor its dwellings as evidence.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:128\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The focus’s أَعْمَالَهُمْ may include monumental building: Hud challenges Âd for raising landmarks on every height for amusement and making works as though they would last. Their visible dwelling traces can thus expose the impermanence of impressive works.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"26:128\",\"ar\":\"أَتَبْنُونَ بِكُلِّ رِيعٍ ءَايَةًۭ تَعْبَثُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:129\",\"ar\":\"وَتَتَّخِذُونَ مَصَانِعَ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَخْلُدُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus names Âd and points to their dwellings; Hud’s address describes Âd’s building activity.\",\"limits\":\"The focus does not specify that أَعْمَالَهُمْ means these buildings or that the visible dwellings are the exact structures in Hud’s rebuke.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:135\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"In the Âd episode, Hud warns of a great day, and they answer that admonition makes no difference. This gives a concrete form to the gap between the focus’s مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ and their being turned from the way.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"26:123\",\"ar\":\"كَذَّبَتْ عَادٌ ٱلْمُرْسَلِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:135\",\"ar\":\"إِنِّىٓ أَخَافُ عَلَيْكُمْ عَذَابَ يَوْمٍ عَظِيمٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:136\",\"ar\":\"قَالُوا۟ سَوَآءٌ عَلَيْنَآ أَوَعَظْتَ أَمْ لَمْ تَكُن مِّنَ ٱلْوَٰعِظِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَكَانُوا۟ مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The target people are Âd, and the focus itself juxtaposes their perception with their diversion.\",\"limits\":\"The response to Hud’s warning does not explain the focus’s attribution of their diversion to Satan.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:141\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Thamud’s account likewise opens with rejection of the messengers, giving the focus’s paired names a shared history of prophetic denial.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"26:141\",\"ar\":\"كَذَّبَتْ ثَمُودُ ٱلْمُرْسَلِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَثَمُودَا۟\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus names Thamud alongside Âd, and this passage names their response to messengers.\",\"limits\":\"The shared rejection formula does not prove the two peoples had identical histories or offences.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:142\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"Salih’s appeal to Thamud asks whether they will not be mindful of God, giving the focus’s “way” a concrete prophetic summons in this account.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"26:142\",\"ar\":\"إِذْ قَالَ لَهُمْ أَخُوهُمْ صَٰلِحٌ أَلَا تَتَّقُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"فَصَدَّهُمْ عَنِ ٱلسَّبِيلِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus names Thamud, while Salih’s address makes a prior summons to God-consciousness explicit.\",\"limits\":\"The opening appeal does not identify which particular deed or belief the focus’s diversion concerns.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:149\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The focus’s reference to Thamud’s dwellings gains a specific architectural counterpart: Salih addresses people who carve skillfully made houses from mountains.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"26:149\",\"ar\":\"وَتَنْحِتُونَ مِنَ ٱلْجِبَالِ بُيُوتًۭا فَٰرِهِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages point to built habitation associated with Thamud.\",\"limits\":\"26:149 describes the houses while occupied; it does not itself say they survived as ruins.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:150\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The account places a demand to fear God and obey Salih immediately after its description of Thamud’s mountain houses, setting skilled habitation beside a prophetic obligation.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"26:149\",\"ar\":\"وَتَنْحِتُونَ مِنَ ٱلْجِبَالِ بُيُوتًۭا فَٰرِهِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:150\",\"ar\":\"فَٱتَّقُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ وَأَطِيعُونِ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:151\",\"ar\":\"وَلَا تُطِيعُوٓا۟ أَمْرَ ٱلْمُسْرِفِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"Thamud’s impressive building and the call governing their conduct occur side by side.\",\"limits\":\"This exhortation does not state why their dwellings later became evidence of their fate.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:157\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Thamud violate the explicit warning about the she-camel and then become remorseful; this portrays recognition arriving after the decisive act, a counterpoint to the focus’s perceptive yet diverted people.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"26:156\",\"ar\":\"وَلَا تَمَسُّوهَا بِسُوٓءٍۢ فَيَأْخُذَكُمْ عَذَابُ يَوْمٍ عَظِيمٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:157\",\"ar\":\"فَعَقَرُوهَا فَأَصْبَحُوا۟ نَٰدِمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَكَانُوا۟ مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both accounts concern Thamud and the relationship between awareness and action.\",\"limits\":\"The focus does not mention the she-camel or say that remorse followed its events.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:158\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Thamud’s punishment is explicitly called a sign; this adds a second description of their aftermath as evidence beside the focus’s testimony from their dwellings.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"26:158\",\"ar\":\"فَأَخَذَهُمُ ٱلْعَذَابُ ۗ إِنَّ فِى ذَٰلِكَ لَءَايَةًۭ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَقَد تَّبَيَّنَ لَكُم مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"The same community’s punished outcome is evidence in both passages.\",\"limits\":\"26:158 does not specify that the sign is the dwellings themselves.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:222\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The focus’s singular ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ may connect to the plural devils who descend on every lying sinner, extending the scene from an agent of beautification to a wider satanic company.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"26:222\",\"ar\":\"تَنَزَّلُ عَلَىٰ كُلِّ أَفَّاكٍ أَثِيمٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:221\",\"ar\":\"هَلْ أُنَبِّئُكُمْ عَلَىٰ مَن تَنَزَّلُ ٱلشَّيَٰطِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَزَيَّنَ لَهُمُ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ\"}],\"missing\":\"The text does not connect Âd or Thamud to these receivers of satanic descent, and the mechanism here is descent rather than beautification; a textual link between the episodes would decide the comparison.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-27:4\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"This passage repeats the pairing زَيَّنَ لَهُمُ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ but attributes the beautification to “We” and says the result is wandering; it confirms the wording’s pattern while showing that 29:38’s explicit Satan attribution is not built into the verb itself.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"27:4\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ ٱلَّذِينَ لَا يُؤْمِنُونَ بِٱلْءَاخِرَةِ زَيَّنَّا لَهُمْ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ فَهُمْ يَعْمَهُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَزَيَّنَ لَهُمُ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ فَصَدَّهُمْ عَنِ ٱلسَّبِيلِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The shared formula makes the different grammatical agents and consequences audible.\",\"limits\":\"27:4 addresses people who disbelieve in the Hereafter; it does not identify them with Âd and Thamud or explain the divine attribution.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-27:24\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"This is a near-verbatim parallel to the focus’s Satan-beautifies-deeds-and-turns-them-from-the-way formula. Its ending says “they are not guided,” while 29:38 ends paradoxically with “they were perceptive.”\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"27:24\",\"ar\":\"وَزَيَّنَ لَهُمُ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ فَصَدَّهُمْ عَنِ ٱلسَّبِيلِ فَهُمْ لَا يَهْتَدُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَزَيَّنَ لَهُمُ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ فَصَدَّهُمْ عَنِ ٱلسَّبِيلِ وَكَانُوا۟ مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The identical wording invites the closing clauses to be compared directly.\",\"limits\":\"The first passage concerns the Queen of Sheba and her people; the shared formula does not make them the historical groups named in the focus.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-27:45\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The focus speaks of Thamud collectively as diverted, while this account says they were two disputing factions; the pairing may complicate how uniform the focus’s judgment sounds.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"27:45\",\"ar\":\"وَلَقَدْ أَرْسَلْنَآ إِلَىٰ ثَمُودَ أَخَاهُمْ صَٰلِحًا أَنِ ٱعْبُدُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ فَإِذَا هُمْ فَرِيقَانِ يَخْتَصِمُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَثَمُودَا۟ وَقَد تَّبَيَّنَ لَكُم مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\"}],\"missing\":\"The passage does not identify which faction was diverted or whether the focus’s plural generalizes over both; the ensuing account of Salih’s opponents and followers could clarify the scope.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-27:51\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"In the Thamud sequence, a plot is followed by the destruction of the plotters and their people; the next verse makes their empty houses visible. This supplies a narrated route from hostile action to the sort of dwelling evidence named in the focus.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"27:45\",\"ar\":\"وَلَقَدْ أَرْسَلْنَآ إِلَىٰ ثَمُودَ أَخَاهُمْ صَٰلِحًا\"},{\"ref\":\"27:51\",\"ar\":\"فَٱنظُرْ كَيْفَ كَانَ عَٰقِبَةُ مَكْرِهِمْ أَنَّا دَمَّرْنَٰهُمْ وَقَوْمَهُمْ أَجْمَعِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"27:52\",\"ar\":\"فَتِلْكَ بُيُوتُهُمْ خَاوِيَةًۢ بِمَا ظَلَمُوٓا۟\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"The Thamud story supplies both the destruction and the visible houses that the focus condenses into a reminder.\",\"limits\":\"27:51 attributes the outcome to plotting and wrongdoing, not to the focus’s specific Satan-beautification wording.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-27:52\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The empty houses left because their inhabitants did wrong are a close counterpart to the focus’s dwellings, which make the fate of Âd and Thamud clear to later observers.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"27:45\",\"ar\":\"إِلَىٰ ثَمُودَ أَخَاهُمْ صَٰلِحًا\"},{\"ref\":\"27:52\",\"ar\":\"فَتِلْكَ بُيُوتُهُمْ خَاوِيَةًۢ بِمَا ظَلَمُوٓا۟ ۗ إِنَّ فِى ذَٰلِكَ لَءَايَةًۭ لِّقَوْمٍۢ يَعْلَمُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَقَد تَّبَيَّنَ لَكُم مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages make abandoned habitation a sign of a community’s outcome.\",\"limits\":\"27:52’s referent is Thamud in its narrative; it does not identify the focus’s separate traces from Âd.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-27:69\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The command to travel and inspect the end of criminals presents observation of past outcomes as a way to learn; the focus enacts that method by directing its audience to dwellings where the fate has become clear.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"27:69\",\"ar\":\"قُلْ سِيرُوا۟ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ فَٱنظُرُوا۟ كَيْفَ كَانَ عَٰقِبَةُ ٱلْمُجْرِمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَقَد تَّبَيَّنَ لَكُم مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both address an audience invited to learn from the visible history of earlier peoples.\",\"limits\":\"27:69 names no particular people or dwellings, so it does not identify the sites mentioned in the focus.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-28:4\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-28:58\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Another passage links a town’s destroyed prosperity to dwellings left largely uninhabited; its shared مَسَاكِنُ language makes the focus’s houses part of a wider image of habitation surviving its people.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"28:58\",\"ar\":\"وَكَمْ أَهْلَكْنَا مِن قَرْيَةٍۭ بَطِرَتْ مَعِيشَتَهَا ۖ فَتِلْكَ مَسَٰكِنُهُمْ لَمْ تُسْكَن مِّنۢ بَعْدِهِمْ إِلَّا قَلِيلًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus’s dwellings occur in a destruction context, as do the dwellings in 28:58.\",\"limits\":\"28:58 does not name Âd or Thamud; its explanation of ruined prosperity cannot be assigned to them from this passage alone.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-28:63\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"Here human leaders say they led others astray as they themselves strayed, which may complement the focus’s account of Satanic diversion with a social chain of misguidance.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"28:63\",\"ar\":\"هَٰٓؤُلَاءِ ٱلَّذِينَ أَغْوَيْنَآ أَغْوَيْنَٰهُمْ كَمَا غَوَيْنَا\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَزَيَّنَ لَهُمُ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ فَصَدَّهُمْ عَنِ ٱلسَّبِيلِ\"}],\"missing\":\"The passages do not identify the speakers in 28:63 with Âd or Thamud or say how human leaders’ influence relates to Satan’s beautification; a direct narrative link could establish whether these describe joined levels of agency.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-28:87\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The warning not to be turned away from God’s signs uses the same صَدَّ عن construction as the focus. It recasts diversion as a danger the Messenger must resist after revelation has come.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"28:87\",\"ar\":\"وَلَا يَصُدُّنَّكَ عَنْ ءَايَٰتِ ٱللَّهِ بَعْدَ إِذْ أُنزِلَتْ إِلَيْكَ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"فَصَدَّهُمْ عَنِ ٱلسَّبِيلِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The shared verb and separation phrase connect the imperative warning to the focus’s past account of people turned away.\",\"limits\":\"The focus names Satan and the path as agent and target; 28:87 leaves the agent implicit and names God’s signs as the target.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-29:5\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-29:28\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The preceding Lot account identifies its people by an act described as unprecedented, while the focus gives no specific deed for Âd or Thamud; the sequence cautions against treating every destroyed community as guilty of the same offence.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"29:28\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّكُمْ لَتَأْتُونَ ٱلْفَٰحِشَةَ مَا سَبَقَكُم بِهَا مِنْ أَحَدٍۢ مِّنَ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَعَادًۭا وَثَمُودَا۟ وَقَد تَّبَيَّنَ لَكُم\"}],\"activation\":\"The Lot narrative precedes the focus’s next named peoples in the same surah’s succession of punished communities.\",\"limits\":\"The focus does not explicitly contrast its two peoples’ acts with Lot’s people’s offence.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-29:29\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Lot’s people are charged with cutting off ٱلسَّبِيلَ, while the focus says Satan turned Âd and Thamud عَنِ ٱلسَّبِيلِ. The shared definite “way” lets the sequence set literal obstruction of travel beside diversion from a path.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"29:29\",\"ar\":\"وَتَقْطَعُونَ ٱلسَّبِيلَ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"فَصَدَّهُمْ عَنِ ٱلسَّبِيلِ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages use the same definite noun within accounts of wrongdoing and judgment.\",\"limits\":\"The wording does not establish that the phrases have the same sense: one charges Lot’s people with cutting off a way, the other describes being turned away from it.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-29:30\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-29:31\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The focus follows a recent announcement that Lot’s town will be destroyed for wrongdoing, and the account soon says a clear sign was left there; it joins the focus’s list to a locally narrated case of judgment and surviving evidence.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"29:31\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّا مُهْلِكُوٓا۟ أَهْلَ هَٰذِهِ ٱلْقَرْيَةِ ۖ إِنَّ أَهْلَهَا كَانُوا۟ ظَٰلِمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:35\",\"ar\":\"وَلَقَد تَّرَكْنَا مِنْهَآ ءَايَةًۢ بَيِّنَةًۭ لِّقَوْمٍۢ يَعْقِلُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَقَد تَّبَيَّنَ لَكُم مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"The Lot episode and its clear sign immediately precede the focus’s reminder from dwellings.\",\"limits\":\"29:31 does not describe those remains or attribute Lot’s town’s fate to Satan’s beautification.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-29:32\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-29:33\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-29:34\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The Lot episode attributes the town’s coming punishment to its people’s defiance; the focus then moves from this cause-and-judgment account to further named peoples and their visible dwelling evidence.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"29:34\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّا مُنزِلُونَ عَلَىٰٓ أَهْلِ هَٰذِهِ ٱلْقَرْيَةِ رِجْزًۭا مِّنَ ٱلسَّمَآءِ بِمَا كَانُوا۟ يَفْسُقُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَعَادًۭا وَثَمُودَا۟ وَقَد تَّبَيَّنَ لَكُم مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"The same surah places the Lot judgment shortly before its Âd and Thamud reminder.\",\"limits\":\"This is a sequence of separate cases; 29:34 does not explain the deeds or agent named in 29:38.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-29:35\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The Lot account says a clear sign was left for people who reason; this is a close local precedent for the focus’s claim that dwellings make the outcome of Âd and Thamud evident.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"29:35\",\"ar\":\"وَلَقَد تَّرَكْنَا مِنْهَآ ءَايَةًۢ بَيِّنَةًۭ لِّقَوْمٍۢ يَعْقِلُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَقَد تَّبَيَّنَ لَكُم مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both refer to clear evidence left from a destroyed community for later observers.\",\"limits\":\"29:35 refers to the Lot account’s sign, not the dwellings of Âd and Thamud.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-29:36\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"Shuʿayb calls Midian to worship God and expect the Last Day; after their rejection and punishment, the focus moves to Âd and Thamud. The transition follows a narrated refusal with a more compressed historical reminder.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"29:36\",\"ar\":\"فَقَالَ يَٰقَوْمِ ٱعْبُدُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ وَٱرْجُوا۟ ٱلْيَوْمَ ٱلْءَاخِرَ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:37\",\"ar\":\"فَكَذَّبُوهُ فَأَخَذَتْهُمُ ٱلرَّجْفَةُ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَعَادًۭا وَثَمُودَا۟\"}],\"activation\":\"The Midian episode is the immediately preceding community account in the surah.\",\"limits\":\"Shuʿayb’s specific appeal is not given as the message Âd or Thamud rejected.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-29:37\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The immediately preceding account shows Midian’s people denying their messenger and ending collapsed in their homes; the focus then points to the dwellings of other peoples as evidence of their fate.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"29:37\",\"ar\":\"فَكَذَّبُوهُ فَأَخَذَتْهُمُ ٱلرَّجْفَةُ فَأَصْبَحُوا۟ فِى دَارِهِمْ جَٰثِمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَقَد تَّبَيَّنَ لَكُم مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"The transition is from one punished people found in their dwelling to a reminder from other peoples’ dwellings.\",\"limits\":\"29:37 does not say that Midian’s dwelling remained as a visible sign or that its punishment had the focus’s cause.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-29:39\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The catalogue after Âd and Thamud adds a group who received clear proofs but became arrogant; this places their perceptiveness beside another failure to respond rightly to evidence.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَكَانُوا۟ مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:39\",\"ar\":\"وَلَقَدْ جَآءَهُم مُّوسَىٰ بِٱلْبَيِّنَٰتِ فَٱسْتَكْبَرُوا۟ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The adjacent catalogue moves from the focus’s perceptive Âd and Thamud to recipients of Moses’s manifest proofs.\",\"limits\":\"29:39 concerns Qarun, Pharaoh, and Haman; it does not say they were diverted by Satan in the same manner.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-29:40\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The next verse says every listed group was seized for its own sin and wronged itself; it gives a judgment frame for the focus’s Satanic diversion without transferring responsibility away from the people.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَعَادًۭا وَثَمُودَا۟\"},{\"ref\":\"29:39\",\"ar\":\"وَقَٰرُونَ وَفِرْعَوْنَ وَهَٰمَٰنَ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:40\",\"ar\":\"فَكُلًّا أَخَذْنَا بِذَنۢبِهِۦ ۖ فَمِنْهُم مَّنْ أَرْسَلْنَا عَلَيْهِ حَاصِبًۭا وَمِنْهُم مَّنْ أَخَذَتْهُ ٱلصَّيْحَةُ وَمِنْهُم مَّنْ خَسَفْنَا بِهِ ٱلْأَرْضَ وَمِنْهُم مَّنْ أَغْرَقْنَا\"},{\"ref\":\"29:40\",\"ar\":\"وَلَٰكِن كَانُوٓا۟ أَنفُسَهُمْ يَظْلِمُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The verse generalizes over the communities just named, including Âd and Thamud.\",\"limits\":\"It gives no individual punishment or specific sin for the two peoples named in the focus.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-29:41\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The spider’s house may echo the focus’s dwellings as a contrast between structures and the protection people expect from them: one set remains as evidence after its people are gone, while the other is explicitly the weakest house.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:41\",\"ar\":\"وَإِنَّ أَوْهَنَ ٱلْبُيُوتِ لَبَيْتُ ٱلْعَنكَبُوتِ\"}],\"missing\":\"There is no shared dwelling term or explicit link between the ruined settlements and the spider-house parable; a surah-level connection between these images could establish whether the contrast is intended.\",\"support\":\"weak\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-29:42\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-29:43\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The surah says its parables are understood only by the knowledgeable; this casts its preceding histories, including the focus’s dwelling evidence, as examples whose significance depends on understanding rather than sight alone.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"29:43\",\"ar\":\"وَتِلْكَ ٱلْأَمْثَٰلُ نَضْرِبُهَا لِلنَّاسِ ۖ وَمَا يَعْقِلُهَآ إِلَّا ٱلْعَٰلِمُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَقَد تَّبَيَّنَ لَكُم مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"وَكَانُوا۟ مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus is one of the surah’s recent historical examples, and it joins manifest traces to a description of the peoples’ perception.\",\"limits\":\"The text does not equate مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ with ٱلْعَٰلِمُونَ or state that the former understood these parables.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-29:44\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-29:54\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"X1\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The Fatiha’s repeated plea for guidance and its naming of the astray provide a daily counter-scene to people turned from the way despite being perceptive: perception alone does not remove the need for guidance.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:7\",\"ar\":\"وَلَا ٱلضَّآلِّينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:38\",\"ar\":\"فَصَدَّهُمْ عَنِ ٱلسَّبِيلِ وَكَانُوا۟ مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages concern the way and the danger of going astray; the Fatiha is recited in every salah.\",\"limits\":\"The path terms differ, and the Fatiha does not identify these historical peoples.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}"}}
